scratchpad/judges/WS5.4-claude/probe.log, probe2.log, probe-remedy.log.

1. PREMISE CHECK: CONFIRMED EXACTLY
Probe: a temp test in my copy only (zz_judge_claude_test.go, since deleted)
POSTs the real form through handleWhatIfSettings -> UpdateSettingsWithPersons
-> prepare.ValidatePersons -> personsSaveErrorMessage -> renderError. I
report the <p> text, HTML-unescaped, which is what the user sees.
  Q1 `Robert "Bob" Smith`, month ""        -> 400 "Invalid person data: persons:
     invalid birth_month for "Robert \"Bob\" Smith": month is required"
     (matches checker-second's body byte for byte)
  Q2 same name, "19711-08" (reachable from the browser) -> 400 "Invalid person data:
     persons: invalid birth_month for "Robert \"Bob\" Smith": invalid month "19711-08""
  U1/U2 control "Robert Bob Smith" -> "Robert Bob Smith needs a birth month" /
     "...'s birth month "19711-08" isn't a valid month (use YYYY-MM)"
Cause: rePersonsInvalidBirthMonth captures `([^"]*)` from a %q-formatted
name. %q turns `"` into `\"`, so the capture stops and the regex misses.
Every match that does succeed returns the ESCAPED form, and the code then
compares that with `p.Name == name` against the RAW submitted names.
Q1 and Q2 are true in themselves and show the name, though with visible
backslashes. checker-second says the same thing. For quote names alone, the
harm is the wording, not a false message.

2. AGAINST RULING g AS WRITTEN
(a) Empty month -> "<Name> needs a birth month". The "(or equivalent — must
name the person and must not say it is missing)" note belongs to the
unparseable clause. "Must not say it is missing" only makes sense there, so
the empty clause gets no such latitude. Oracle P3c and the worker's own tests
both check for the phrase "needs a birth month". Q1 does not produce it. The
lead's point that this case is "reachable only by a direct POST" does not
separate Q1 from anything else: the UI field is `required`, so EVERY
empty-month case is direct-POST-only. The clause was written for exactly that
path, and the contract makes no exception for names that %q escapes.
(b) The headline rule is "Word every persons/start-date 400 from the SUBMITTED
value". For any name %q escapes (`"`, `\`, tab, NBSP and other characters Go
does not treat as printable), the classifier cannot find the submitted row:
  B1 `Pat\X` ""       -> "Pat\\X's birth month isn't valid (use YYYY-MM)"
  N1 "Ann<NBSP>Lee" "" -> "Ann Lee's birth month isn't valid (use YYYY-MM)"
  T1 "Ann<TAB>Lee" ""  -> "Ann\tLee's birth month isn't valid (use YYYY-MM)"
  T2 `Sam": X` ""     -> "Sam\'s birth month isn't valid (use YYYY-MM)"
In each of these the month is empty but the message does not say so, and the
name shown is not the person's name (T2 shows only a truncated fragment).
None of this is worded from the submitted value.
(c) "Other persons errors stay truthful" is the hard-stop's core requirement
and the ground the lead would overrule on. The same round-trip produces
FALSE messages:
  C3 A=`Pat\X` "19711-08" (the five-digit-year typo from attempt 3, reachable
     in the browser), B=`Pat\\X` "1990-01" -> 400 "Pat\\X's birth month
     "1990-01" isn't a valid month". 1990-01 IS valid, it is the other
     person's value, and the real bad value 19711-08 is never mentioned.
  C1 (A empty, direct POST) -> the same false message.
  T3 `Sam": X` "19711-08" + `Sam\` "1990-01" -> "Sam\'s birth month "1990-01"
     isn't a valid month". Again false, and about the wrong person.
These names are contrived, so they count as corroboration, not as the main
ground. They do refute the idea that the defect is only polish. The quote-name
fallback and the false messages come from one bug: the person is identified
by comparing an escaped string with raw names. That regex is new in this
attempt and was written to implement ruling g, so it is squarely in scope.

3. REMEDY CHECK: THE FAIL'S REMEDY WORKS
Throwaway prototype in my copy: regex `("(?:[^"\\]|\\.)*")` plus
strconv.Unquote(m[1]), 3 lines changed. All 16 probes then give the
contract's wording with the right name. Q1 -> "Robert "Bob" Smith needs a
birth month". Q2 -> "...'s birth month "19711-08" isn't a valid month". C3 ->
"Pat\X's birth month "19711-08" isn't a valid month", now the correct person
and value. B1/N1/T1/T2 -> "<real name> needs a birth month".
`go test ./internal/handlers/whatif/ -run 'Glide|Person|PersonsSaveErrorMessage|PersonsValidation'`
-> ok. Prototype discarded. `cmp` shows handlers_rates.go restored identical
to the snapshot.

RULING: UPHOLD. The factual premise is exact. For names the unrestricted name
field accepts, the empty-month clause of ruling g and its headline rule
("word from the SUBMITTED value") are not met. The mechanism the FAIL names
also breaks the truthfulness requirement the lead relies on (C3, reachable in
the browser). The fix is small, stays in scope, and has been verified to
work. Out-of-contract note for the backlog, not part of this ruling:
checker-tests F1 (duplicate-name hedge) is separate and truthful.
